# Optional: enables the Claude summary and fix suggestions on each report
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Model used for triage (defaults to claude-opus-5-5)
CLAUDE_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5
# How hard Claude thinks when writing a summary: low, medium (default), high or max. Lower uses fewer credits.
# A re-test that finds exactly the same issues reuses the last summary and uses no credits at all.
# CLAUDE_EFFORT=medium
# Only for running without Docker (Docker always uses port 4000 and /data inside the container)
# PORT=4000
# DATA_DIR=./data
# How many scans run at the same time
# Tests run at the same time when the owner has not set it (Owner panel > Settings, 1 to 8)
SCAN_CONCURRENCY=2
# Pages a website test opens at the same time, each with all its agents (1 to 16, default 4).
# The owner panel can change it too. Sites that answer "429 too many requests" drop to one page at a time.
# PARALLEL_PAGES=4
# Docker image processor type: linux/amd64 (default, servers and Apple Silicon via Rosetta)
# or linux/arm64 for ARM Linux servers
# DOCKER_PLATFORM=linux/amd64
# Login switch. 1 = login off: no owner account or sign-in, only for your own computer while building.
# docker compose uses 1 when this is not set. Change it to 0 before putting the system on a server.
AUTH_DISABLED=1
# Key that encrypts saved client logins (website sign-ins, API keys, Git tokens). Required on a server.
# Make one with: openssl rand -base64 32   Keep a copy: if it is lost, saved logins must be entered again.
# Without it, a key is made once and kept in the data folder (fine on your own computer).
# SECRET_KEY=
# Optional: create the owner account on first start without opening the dashboard
# OWNER_EMAIL=
# OWNER_PASSWORD=
# Set to 1 when running behind Nginx or Caddy, so secure cookies and client addresses work
# TRUST_PROXY=1
# Allow testing localhost and private network addresses (internal staging sites)
# ALLOW_PRIVATE_TARGETS=1
# This server's address as the client's app sees it, for the webhook inbox (like https://test.glvinfotech.com)
# PUBLIC_URL=
# Outside tools (tabs on the new-test page). Docker installs Lighthouse, the W3C HTML checker,
# testssl.sh, Nuclei, Schemathesis, Newman, Gitleaks, Opengrep, OSV-Scanner, Trivy, k6 and JMeter
# in the image, and runs OWASP ZAP next to it. k6, JMeter, ZAP's active scan and GLV's attack probes
# run only for a project with the client's written permission (see README.md).
# Key between the app and its ZAP container. Set your own random value on a server:
# ZAP_API_KEY=
# Tests started without a tool choice (the API, old projects) run every installed safe tool.
# Set to none to run only the platform's own checks unless tools are chosen.
# DEFAULT_TOOLS=none
# Without Docker: where the tools are, when they are not on PATH
# ZAP_URL=http://localhost:8080
# NUCLEI_PATH=/usr/local/bin/nuclei
# TESTSSL_PATH=/usr/bin/testssl
# Minutes the TLS check (testssl.sh) may take before it counts as not finished (default 15)
# TESTSSL_MINUTES=15
# GITLEAKS_PATH=/usr/local/bin/gitleaks
# OPENGREP_PATH=/usr/local/bin/opengrep
# OSV_SCANNER_PATH=/usr/local/bin/osv-scanner
# TRIVY_PATH=/usr/local/bin/trivy
# SCHEMATHESIS_PATH=/usr/local/bin/st
# NEWMAN_PATH=/usr/local/bin/newman
# K6_PATH=/usr/local/bin/k6
# JMETER_PATH=/opt/jmeter/bin/jmeter
# Folder of Opengrep rules (default: GLV's own rules in rules/opengrep)
# OPENGREP_RULES=./rules/opengrep
# Where Trivy keeps its vulnerability database (default: trivy-cache in the data folder)
# TRIVY_CACHE_DIR=
# 1 = Trivy uses the database it already has and does not update it (for servers without internet;
# copy a trivy-cache folder into TRIVY_CACHE_DIR first)
# TRIVY_OFFLINE=1
# 1 = OSV-Scanner checks packages against a local copy of the OSV database instead of asking osv.dev
# for each one (the copy is downloaded once into OSV_SCANNER_LOCAL_DB_CACHE_DIRECTORY)
# OSV_SCANNER_OFFLINE=1
# OSV_SCANNER_LOCAL_DB_CACHE_DIRECTORY=

# Optional modules in their own containers (Owner panel > Modules). Start the profile, add its
# line here, then restart the app (docker compose up -d).
# Test mailbox: docker compose --profile mail up -d
# MAILPIT_URL=http://mailpit:8025
# Domain of the addresses journeys use for {{inbox}} (any domain works: Mailpit takes every email)
# MAIL_DOMAIN=inbox.glv.test
# Live load charts: docker compose --profile loadcharts up -d
# K6_INFLUX_URL=http://influxdb:8086
# GRAFANA_URL=http://localhost:3000
# GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Client connections (project > Connections tab): the client's server over SSH, their Hostinger
# account, their MCP server. Secrets pasted on the tab are kept encrypted with SECRET_KEY. To keep a
# secret out of the database, write a line here whose name starts with CONN_ and use env:CONN_NAME on
# the tab (only CONN_ names can be read, so a connection never sends this server's own keys anywhere).
# Keys with line breaks can be written on one line with \n.
# CONN_ACME_HOSTINGER_TOKEN=
# CONN_ACME_MCP_TOKEN=
# SSH key files for file:name on the tab (default: ssh-keys in the data folder, /data/ssh-keys in Docker)
# SSH_KEYS_DIR=
# Hostinger API address (only change it for a test stand-in)
# HOSTINGER_API_URL=https://developers.hostinger.com

# Wave 1 modules (2026 gap report). Each is used only when its line is set.
# Blind (out-of-band) holes in the attack agent: GLV's own public address, which the client's server
# calls back (http://<this>/oob/<token>). Without it, blind checks are skipped.
# OOB_BASE_URL=https://glv-test.example.com
# Or an Interactsh server (docker compose --profile oob up -d, needs its own domain), for DNS
# callbacks and Log4Shell:
# INTERACTSH_SERVER=https://oob.example.com
# INTERACTSH_TOKEN=
# OOB_DOMAIN=oob.example.com
# Browser version matrix: docker compose --profile grid up -d
# SELENIUM_GRID_URL=http://selenium-hub:4444
# Mobile security: docker compose --profile mobsf up -d (API key on MobSF's API docs page, port 8000)
# MOBSF_URL=http://mobsf:8000
# MOBSF_API_KEY=
# Phone farm: docker compose --profile farm up -d on the machine the phones are plugged into
# STF_URL=http://stf:7100
# STF_TOKEN=
# FARM_PUBLIC_IP=
# iPhones on USB (go-ios, in the image), and a separate Appium for iPhones if you run one
# GO_IOS_PATH=ios
# IOS_APPIUM_URL=
# DNS server for the email and DNS checks (default: the system's)
# DNS_SERVER=1.1.1.1
# Extra YARA rules folder for the hacked-site scan (default: GLV's rules/yara)
# YARA_RULES=
# Plain-English test writing uses Claude when ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is set; set this to off to use only
# GLV's own rules
# PLAIN_TESTS_AI=off

# Wave 2 modules (2026 gap report). Each is optional and used only when installed/set.
# SBOM: Syft gives a fuller SBOM than GLV's own lockfile reader (bundled in the image).
# SYFT_PATH=syft
# Cloud account posture (Prowler). Install prowler in the image (pip venv) and point here; the
# client's READ-ONLY keys go in CONN_<name>_… lines (see the cloud tool's hint).
# PROWLER_PATH=prowler
# API mocks: Prism gives a richer mock than GLV's own (optional).
# PRISM_PATH=prism
# Maestro app flows (needs a connected phone): curl -Ls https://get.maestro.mobile.dev | bash
# MAESTRO_PATH=maestro
# Speed from other regions: one line per region, pointing at a cheap VPS proxy in that region.
# REGION_MUMBAI_PROXY=http://mumbai-vps:3128
# REGION_SINGAPORE_PROXY=http://singapore-vps:3128
# REGION_FRANKFURT_PROXY=http://frankfurt-vps:3128
# Client cloud keys for the cloud posture tool (read-only), e.g. a SecurityAudit AWS user:
# CONN_ACME_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# CONN_ACME_AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Wave 3 modules (2026 gap report, P3). All optional and used only when installed/set.
# Model file security: ModelScan deepens GLV's own pickle-format check (pip install modelscan).
# MODELSCAN_PATH=modelscan
# Infrastructure-as-code: Checkov deepens GLV's own IaC rules (pip install checkov).
# CHECKOV_PATH=checkov
# Kubernetes/Docker host checks use kube-bench/Kubescape/Dockle and GoAccess ON THE CLIENT'S SERVER
# over SSH when installed there; nothing to set here.
# Post-quantum TLS probe needs OpenSSL 3.5+ on PATH (bundled). Backlinks query a Common Crawl mirror
# (default index CC-MAIN-2026-05); point the tool's option at a GLV mirror for in-house use.

# Wave 4 modules (the previously-deferred tail, now in-house/free). Each is configured from its options
# in the UI, not from env — nothing below is required. Notes on what the OPERATOR stands up (all free):
# - Voice-bot testing needs open-source Whisper and Piper on PATH (pip install openai-whisper; Piper
#   release binary). The tool shows "install Whisper/Piper" until they are present.
# - Rank tracking: run a self-hosted SearXNG (free) and paste its URL, or the client's own free Google
#   Custom Search key + cx. AI visibility: run a local Ollama (free) and paste its /v1 URL, or the
#   client's own key. Both are directional, not identical to the paid tools.
# - Windows browsers: run a free Windows evaluation VM with msedgedriver and paste its WebDriver URL.
# - Push send uses the client's OWN FCM/APNs key and runs only under the "changes" permission.
# - Jira/Linear export uses the client's OWN tracker token (or produces a CSV).
# WHISPER_PATH and PIPER_PATH are auto-detected from PATH; set them only to override.
# WHISPER_PATH=whisper
# PIPER_PATH=piper
